Other important topics

1. The Core equals()–hashCode() Contract
Ans:
If two objects are equal according to equals(), they MUST have the same hashCode()
But the reverse is not required

equals()
- The equals(Object obj) method determines logical equality, not reference equality.

Rules of equals()
1. Reflexive
x.equals(x) == true

2. Symmetric
x.equals(y) == true  ⇒  y.equals(x) == true

3. Transitive
x.equals(y) == true && y.equals(z) == true ⇒ x.equals(z) == true

4. Consistent
Multiple calls must return the same result if the objects don’t change.

5. Non-null
x.equals(null) == false

2. hashCode() Contract
hashCode() returns an integer used by hash-based collections to determine bucket placement.

1. Consistent
- Multiple calls return the same value as long as object state doesn’t change.

2. Equal objects must have equal hash codes
if x.equals(y) == true
then x.hashCode() == y.hashCode() must be true

3. Unequal objects may have same hash code
This is allowed (called a hash collision).
